 "source_type": "instagram",
 "source_url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DcUk326hg5R/",
 "title": "Why keep your worst salesperson?",
 "topic": "sales",
 "summary": "The creator explains why his dad refused to fire a low-performing car salesman when he was growing up. The main lesson is that a team member who creates a positive, drama-free culture and lifts everyone around them can be more valuable than a top producer who makes everyone miserable.",
 "key_points": [
  "The creator's dad owned a small car dealership and kept a salesman who could not really sell anything.",
  "When asked why he didn't fire him, the dad explained that everybody was happier when he was around because he was always smiling, positive, super loyal, trusted, and never created drama.",
  "The dad said he was paying him not just for the cars he sold, but for what he did to the room.",
  "The dad stated he would rather have someone who produces a little less and makes everyone around him better, than a top producer who makes everyone around him miserable.",
  "The creator concludes that your highest producer is not always your most valuable person.",
  "Do not only measure what someone produces, measure what their presence produces.",
  "Some people increase revenue, some people increase everybody around them, and the rare ones do both."
 ],
 "content": "Instagram reel/post by Amir Syed. Text on screen: Why my dad refused to fire his worst salesman. Transcript: My dad once taught me why the worst salesperson on your team might be worth keeping. Growing up, my dad owned a small car dealership. He had this one sales guy, great guy, but couldn't really sell anything. And I never understood why my dad kept him around. So one day, on our drive home, I asked my dad, why don't you just fire him? And my dad said something I'll never forget. He said, son, because everybody's happier when he's around. He was always smiling, always positive, super loyal. People trusted him. He never created drama. And when he walked into work, he brought life into the business. Here's what I didn't understand back then. My dad wasn't just paying him for the cars he sold. He was paying him for what he did to the room. He basically told me, son, I'd rather have someone who produces a little less and makes everyone around him better than a top producer who makes everyone around him miserable. That's what stuck with me all these years. Because years later, after building my own companies, I finally understand it. Your highest producer isn't always your most valuable person. Don't only measure what someone produces, measure what their presence produces. Some people increase revenue, some people increase everybody around them, the rare ones do both. And those people, you never let them go. Click follow, I share the business lessons I've had to live to learn, let's go. Text shown on screen: Your worst salesperson might be worth keeping.",
